Computation Types, Effects, Coeffects, and Inferred Regions
===============================================================================

1. Objective and authority
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Programs should express recursive data construction, field access, matching,
iteration, and parallel reduction with ordinary Mech forms. The compiler should
infer the storage obligations needed to execute them safely. Explicit memory
controls remain an optional way to state resource constraints.

The benchmarks-game directive applies throughout this work: algorithms stay in
Mech; new operations and optimizations must generalize across programs. There
is no tree, nbody, EKF, or other domain recognition in semantic analysis or
allocation. Benchmark construction and traversal must remain measurable work.

Schema and validated ShapeInstance remain the semantic type authority. The
type-memory contract describes obligations. The memory planner chooses capacity,
layout, lifetimes, and reuse; MemoryDomain and RealizedMemoryPlan realize that
plan. Computation facts do not introduce another allocator or another schema
identity. Runtime capabilities remain unforgeable; inferred requirements are
compiler facts, not permission tokens.

Relevant existing contracts:
- docs/design/semantic-type-authority.md
- docs/design/type-system-v1.md
- docs/design/type-memory-boundary.md
- docs/design/memory-planner.md
- docs/design/memory-runtime.md

2. Distinct semantic questions
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Value types describe data structure, nullability, and any observable identity.
Effects describe what evaluating an expression may do to observable state.
Coeffects describe what its calling context must provide.
Lifetime and escape facts describe which values remain reachable and when.
Resource bounds describe how much planned storage a computation needs.

These facts must compose through calls and control flow. They must not collapse
into one Boolean called pure. A pure value may require physical allocation.
An explicitly identity-bearing reference must not be folded or shared merely
because its allocation has no IO effect. Effect ordering remains source ordering;
set-valued may-effect summaries do not authorize reordering or common-subexpression
elimination of calls.

3. First implemented slice: native computation summaries
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The experimental native control IR now infers per-function and entry summaries:
- observable entry input, output, and scheduling effects;
- ambient arena requirements for read, allocate, and frame retirement;
- the source layout required by a managed read or allocation;
- whether a computation uses managed memory, including locally supplied memory;
- whether a computation may allocate, without claiming a count or bound.

Inference computes a deterministic least fixed point over the admitted finite
fact set. Recursive and mutually recursive calls propagate facts to every caller.
Matches, loop callbacks, reductions, argument expressions, and unused statements
participate. Branch facts are conservative unions; this stage does not prove
conditions unreachable or reason about path correlations.

A full arena supplies matching requirements only for its callback. Capacity and
argument expressions execute outside that arena. A frame requires an existing
arena and cannot supply it. Equal field counts do not establish layout equality.
An incompatible inner arena is recorded as a conflict; a matching outer arena
cannot repair it because the inner arena replaces the active context.
Locally supplied arenas discharge resource requirements but cannot discharge IO
or scheduling effects.

Independent parallel callbacks must have no host effects and no unmet ambient
arena requirements. They may create private arenas. Coordinator arenas cannot
be inherited by workers. Streaming callbacks retain their existing restriction
against managed storage. Null is a pure value requiring no arena.

This replaces the native compiler's ad hoc callback traversal. Summaries are
retained on the typed native Program for subsequent analyses. Existing native
transforms refresh them before use; context-dependent native entries consume
the inferred layout requirements. Existing native execution and effect sequencing
are unchanged. Missing callees, undeclared IO
primitives, and unknown managed actions fail analysis.

This is NOT a complete language-wide effect/coeffect type system. It is
monomorphic, uses the native IR's admitted layouts, and has no region variables,
typed link provenance, inferred reclamation, effect polymorphism, or capacity
inference. Entry operations outside an arena still receive existing runtime
guards. Only worker resource independence is newly enforced at compilation.

4. Milestones and acceptance criteria
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(4.1) Computation facts and operation contracts -- started

The native summary and callback validation slice above is implemented.
Next, express general operation effects and contextual requirements alongside
KindScheme/operation declarations and attach facts to authoritative semantic HIR.
Carry facts through semantic binding without selecting semantics from storage.
Define region variables, instantiation, and structured source diagnostics.

Acceptance: direct, transitive, and mutually recursive effects agree; unknown
contracts cannot be treated as pure; private resources discharge only correctly
scoped obligations; IO order and native objects remain unchanged for admitted
programs. Facts are consumed by a real compiler decision, not merely printed.

(4.2) Recursive semantic values and typed links -- next major milestone

Specify finite recursive value graphs within the single Schema authority,
including canonical encoding, equality, nullability, and nominal versus structural
identity. Extend schema validation and type-memory contracts together. KindExpr
already has Reference and Option forms, but SchemaBody has no corresponding
recursive link representation; a new native-only kind enum is insufficient.

Start with immutable, acyclic construction. Use existing canonical declarations,
record literals, match forms, and field access wherever possible. Resolve the
precise source type spelling from canonical grammar and semantic commitments
before adding syntax. Distinguish optional payloads from null handles. Preserve
typed pointee information and reject incompatible fields or links at compilation.

Acceptance: non-tree linked records and recursive trees compile using ordinary
construction and typed field access; opaque ref and explicit typed reads are
unnecessary for the ordinary path; malformed recursive schemas and illegal
nullable access produce structured errors; codecs and schema identity agree.

(4.3) Region, escape, and retention inference

Track allocation provenance through record fields, calls, return values, matches,
loops, and captures. Infer region obligations separately from physical placement.
Distinguish a consumed temporary graph from a graph returned to its caller or
retained by state, feedback, a published output, or an incremental cache.
Compute recursive function summaries to a fixed point or conservatively retain
storage when escape information cannot be proved.

Acceptance: temporary construction consumed to a scalar is reclaimed at its
proved boundary; returned data stays live; the long-lived benchmark tree remains
live through all temporary work; references cannot outlive their owners. Apply
the same rules to non-tree record graphs and caller-owned values. No syntax
shape, function name, or benchmark identifier selects a region strategy.

(4.4) Resource bounds and existing planner integration

Infer checked allocation bounds from ordinary expression and recurrence facts
where possible. When a bound is unavailable, use an explicit measured witness
and revised plan under the existing planner/runtime contract. Never interpret
unknown as zero or silently grow a fixed reservation. Expose source diagnostics
or an optional general budget constraint when no admitted plan is available.

Lower inferred region obligations to existing memory plans. Reuse storage only
after escape, retention, alias, and effect obligations permit it. Allocation
failure and overflow remain checked. Physical allocation counts remain distinct
from semantic type identity.

Acceptance: ordinary source does not need the tree capacity formula or frame
callback wrappers; capacity overflow and exhaustion have deterministic behavior;
reuse is validated against retained values; all existing memory contract tests
pass. Benchmark validation verifies complete construction and traversal.

(4.5) Parallel comprehensions and capture analysis

Infer free variables and contextual requirements for a general parallel consumer
of a comprehension. Transfer copied immutable values or proven safe shared reads;
reject conflicting writes and borrowed capabilities that cannot cross workers.
Give worker-local inferred regions independent realizations and planned reuse.
Reduction scheduling must preserve the language's ordering and numeric rules.

Acceptance: range callbacks, manual tuple captures, and worker arena wrappers are
unnecessary for the benchmark; non-tree parallel workloads pass isolation and
capture tests; the algorithm remains in Mech. Compare complete implementations
and report repeated full-size timing and source-size results.

(4.6) Broader language rollout

Add mutation and ownership permissions, cyclic graphs, effect/coeffect scheme
polymorphism, retained reactive values and turn transactions, closure escape,
serialization/publishing boundaries, and non-native backend lowering as separate
reviewable extensions. GPU placement remains the compute planner's authority;
effects and resource facts constrain admissible placement rather than replacing
that planner. External transactional effect journals are a separate runtime
protocol and must not be mistaken for source computation-type inference.

5. Planning estimate
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These are rough engineer-effort estimates, not delivery dates:
- computation facts and semantic operation contracts: 1-2 weeks;
- recursive schemas, typing, and native representation: 2-3 weeks;
- escape/retention inference and initial planner lowering: 2-3 weeks;
- bounds, parallel captures, and benchmark validation: 1-2 weeks.

The first ordinary-source native benchmark target is approximately 6-10
engineer-weeks. A language-wide rollout including mutation, polymorphism,
reactivity, codecs, and multiple backends is a multi-month project, provisionally
3-6 months. Re-estimate after the recursive schema design and the first region
inference prototype; those contain the largest unresolved semantic decisions.

Completion is determined by the acceptance criteria, not source line count alone.
The approximately 0.7-second Rust arena measurement is the performance target,
not a promise that a syntax or typing change will automatically meet it.
